new='**2. Weekly stayer (Wochenaufenthalter) and others who lose commuter status.** After actual commuter status fails, test art. 15(2) before allocating Swiss work pay under art. 15(1): pay remains taxable only in the residence state if presence in the other state is not more than 183 days in the relevant calendar year, the actual treaty employer is not resident there, and its permanent establishment there does not bear the remuneration. Count presence days, not just working days; establish employer and any economic-employer issue from the actual facts. An ordinary German-resident non-commuter present for 80 days in Switzerland for an actual German treaty employer, with no Swiss permanent establishment bearing the pay and all other stated gates met, stays exclusively German-taxable on that pay. At 184 days or with a Swiss-resident employer, that exception fails; then art. 15(1), subject to the other actual special rules, allows Swiss taxation of pay for work there. Germany exempts qualifying pay with progression (art. 24(1) no. 1(c), no. 2; § 32b(1) no. 3 EStG), only on proof either that Switzerland waived its taxing right or that Swiss tax assessed on the pay was paid (§ 50d(8)); a bare zero-withholding certificate does not itself establish a waiver. Under that ordinary art. 15(1) workday route, pay for days outside Switzerland stays German-taxable. Existing examples with a Swiss employer do not meet art. 15(2)(b).'
